  "practice": [
    {
      "record_id": "class_7_106_practice_90_GENERATED_0001_9655bab3f293be69cdff",
      "chapter_id": "106",
      "chapter_title": "THE STATE THE GOVERNMENT AND YOU",
      "pillar": "practice",
      "asset": "90_GENERATED",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Should the Parliament be the most powerful institution in the country? Why or why not?",
      "visibility": "student",
      "student_facing": true,
      "content_origin": "GENERATED",
      "status": "GENERATED",
      "source": {
        "file": "Contents\\Class 7\\03_SOCIAL_SCIENCE_GRADE7_PART2_COMPLETE\\03_SOCIAL_SCIENCE_GRADE7_PART2\\106_THE_STATE_THE_GOVERNMENT_AND_YOU\\02_PRACTICE\\90_GENERATED\\GENERATED_PRACTICE.json",
        "source_ref": "GENERATED_PRACTICE.json::record:1",
        "source_page": null,
        "section": ""
      },
      "quality": {
        "is_fragment": false,
        "is_ocr_suspect": false
      },
      "sequence": 1,
      "answer": "The answer depends entirely on whether the Constitution has been amended. Under Article 243, a State Assembly (Parliament at the state level) cannot override any constitutional provision. This ensures that no single branch becomes too powerful and prevents the concentration of power in one institution. Therefore, with this amendment, Parliament is not the most powerful; it is just as balanced as the state governments. Without Amendment: Without this specific clause, a strong central government could theoretically make decisions that disadvantage states or citizens more effectively than local bodies might be able to resist."
    },
    {
      "record_id": "class_7_106_practice_90_GENERATED_0002_6eec62bb71e995c8fd06",
      "chapter_id": "106",
      "chapter_title": "THE STATE THE GOVERNMENT AND YOU",
      "pillar": "practice",
      "asset": "90_GENERATED",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Should the Parliament have the power to make any law it wants, even if it affects your rights?",
      "visibility": "student",
      "student_facing": true,
      "content_origin": "GENERATED",
      "status": "GENERATED",
      "source": {
        "file": "Contents\\Class 7\\03_SOCIAL_SCIENCE_GRADE7_PART2_COMPLETE\\03_SOCIAL_SCIENCE_GRADE7_PART2\\106_THE_STATE_THE_GOVERNMENT_AND_YOU\\02_PRACTICE\\90_GENERATED\\GENERATED_PRACTICE.json",
        "source_ref": "GENERATED_PRACTICE.json::record:2",
        "source_page": null,
        "section": ""
      },
      "quality": {
        "is_fragment": false,
        "is_ocr_suspect": false
      },
      "sequence": 2,
      "answer": "No. Even if Parliament wanted to pass any law (like a 'Romeo and Juliet Act'), you would still be protected by the Constitution. The fundamental rights enshrined in the Preamble of the Indian Constitution provide a shield against arbitrary actions by any legislature. Citizens have inherent rights, and laws cannot arbitrarily infringe upon them."
    },
    {
      "record_id": "class_7_106_practice_90_GENERATED_0003_de32ff86b12044cb2e77",
      "chapter_id": "106",
      "chapter_title": "THE STATE THE GOVERNMENT AND YOU",
      "pillar": "practice",
      "asset": "90_GENERATED",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "What could go wrong if lawmaking has no limits? Can it lead to unfair or unjust laws?",
      "visibility": "student",
      "student_facing": true,
      "content_origin": "GENERATED",
      "status": "GENERATED",
      "source": {
        "file": "Contents\\Class 7\\03_SOCIAL_SCIENCE_GRADE7_PART2_COMPLETE\\03_SOCIAL_SCIENCE_GRADE7_PART2\\106_THE_STATE_THE_GOVERNMENT_AND_YOU\\02_PRACTICE\\90_GENERATED\\GENERATED_PRACTICE.json",
        "source_ref": "GENERATED_PRACTICE.json::record:3",
        "source_page": null,
        "section": ""
      },
      "quality": {
        "is_fragment": false,
        "is_ocr_suspect": false
      },
      "sequence": 3,
      "answer": "It could lead to unfair and unjust laws that favor a specific political agenda at the expense of individual liberties. Without legislative limits, politicians would make decisions based on temporary whims rather than established rules, potentially creating a tyranny of will where no one is truly guaranteed their safety or rights."
    },
    {
      "record_id": "class_7_106_practice_90_GENERATED_0004_2ceabbd289be53d679e3",
      "chapter_id": "106",
      "chapter_title": "THE STATE THE GOVERNMENT AND YOU",
      "pillar": "practice",
      "asset": "90_GENERATED",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Who is more powerful in India, the Parliament or the Supreme Court? Give your reasons.",
      "visibility": "student",
      "student_facing": true,
      "content_origin": "GENERATED",
      "status": "GENERATED",
      "source": {
        "file": "Contents\\Class 7\\03_SOCIAL_SCIENCE_GRADE7_PART2_COMPLETE\\03_SOCIAL_SCIENCE_GRADE7_PART2\\106_THE_STATE_THE_GOVERNMENT_AND_YOU\\02_PRACTICE\\90_GENERATED\\GENERATED_PRACTICE.json",
        "source_ref": "GENERATED_PRACTICE.json::record:4",
        "source_page": null,
        "section": ""
      },
      "quality": {
        "is_fragment": false,
        "is_ocr_suspect": false
      },
      "sequence": 4,
      "answer": "The Supreme Court is generally considered to be the most powerful institution among these two. Reasons for Supreme Court's Power: The Constitution explicitly grants the Supreme Court the power of interpretation. Unlike other judicial bodies in India, the Supreme Court does not follow a strict code of procedure; its decisions are based solely on the facts presented and the rules of evidence. This allows judges to strike down laws that are unconstitutional without needing specific procedural hurdles. Reasons for Parliament's Power: In theory, Parliament has the sole legislative authority (Article 79). However, because the Supreme Court can declare a law unconstitutional via its own review, its power effectively limits the legislature's ability to pass truly unchecked laws."
    }
  ],
